{"reference":"Acts 1","book":"Acts","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Acts begins by resuming an earlier account addressed to Theophilus, making it most natural to read this work as a sequel to Luke. The shared features of the two works strongly support common authorship or coordinated production, though questions about their exact literary relationship remain open. Acts itself never names its narrator, and Theophilus’s role cannot be determined from the text alone.","verse_refs":["Acts 1:1-2","Acts 1:1-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_001_60cb987cadb61f9339664b65","reader_path_acts_001_ede3a72f00b03cc16fa2ed3e","reader_path_acts_001_6f8663e1da29590a35c049b7"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"The Chapter’s Program","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Jesus does not answer the disciples’ question about restoring the kingdom to Israel by setting a timetable. Instead, he redirects their attention to receiving the Spirit and becoming witnesses. The movement from Jerusalem to Judea and Samaria and then to the earth’s end gives the book a major organizing pattern: witness begins where the disciples are and extends outward. This redirection does not declare Israel irrelevant; it shifts the immediate focus from knowing times to carrying out witness.","verse_refs":["Acts 1:6-8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_001_cf9d35a311456137225cb9bf","reader_path_acts_001_556f23a0a8a3890bfb460694"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Ascension Account","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The ascent, cloud, and heavenly messengers present the event through biblical imagery associated with divine appearance rather than explaining a modern physical mechanism. Comparing this account with Luke’s ending is useful: Luke 24 has textual variation concerning the ascension and worship, while Acts describes a renewed period of appearances before the ascent. The two endings should be compared rather than silently forced into a single account.","verse_refs":["Acts 1:1-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_001_cf9d35a311456137225cb9bf","reader_path_acts_001_6f8663e1da29590a35c049b7"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"The Waiting Community","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The group waiting in Jerusalem includes both the named apostles and “the women,” along with Mary and Jesus’ brothers. Acts later portrays women in varied active roles, even though its narration remains selective and shaped by patriarchal constraints. Their presence here helps identify the waiting community as broader than the list of eleven apostles.","verse_refs":["Acts 1:14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_001_bf82adfd279c6a3a20d00154"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Matthias is not simply appointed by Peter. The group interprets Scripture, sets forward two qualified candidates, prays, and casts lots; Matthias is then counted among the eleven. The stated qualification is especially important: the replacement must have accompanied the group from John’s baptism through Jesus’ ascension and so be a witness to the resurrection.","verse_refs":["Acts 1:15-26"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_001_ede3a72f00b03cc16fa2ed3e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Acts as a Whole","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Acts is preserved in early papyri and major Greek manuscripts that contain different portions and textual profiles. One distinctive stream, often called the Western text and represented especially by Codex Bezae and related witnesses, has readings that require evaluation case by case. More broadly, historical questions about Acts are best considered by comparing particular claims with independent evidence rather than treating the entire work as either uniformly reliable or uniformly unreliable.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_001_e0ae5cc0402d9d60cf9ce64c","reader_path_acts_001_ef1648977597084b5529591a","reader_path_acts_001_c34213063b45270439315cc5"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}